public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
    List<List<Integer>> permutations = new ArrayList<>();
    if (nums.length == 1) {
        permutations.add(Arrays.asList(nums[0]));
    } else {
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            int n = nums[i];
            int[] remainingNums = new int[nums.length - 1];
            for (int j = 0, k = 0; j < nums.length; j++) {
                if (j != i) {
                    remainingNums[k++] = nums[j];
                }
            }
            List<List<Integer>> recursivePermutations = permute(remainingNums);
            for (List<Integer> permutation : recursivePermutations) {
                permutation.add(0, n);
                permutations.add(permutation);
            }
        }
    }
    return permutations;
}
```
In this solution, we use recursion to generate all possible permutations of the input array. The idea is to fix one element and then recursively generate all possible permutations of the remaining elements. We then combine the fixed element with each of the possible permutations of the remaining elements to get all possible permutations of the original array.

Base case: If the input array has only one element, the only possible permutation is the array itself, so we return a list containing the array.

Recursive case: If the input array has more than one element, we fix one element and recursively call the `permute` function with the remaining elements. We then combine the fixed element with each of the possible permutations of the remaining elements to get all possible permutations of the original array.

The time and space complexity of this solution are O(n!) and O(n), respectively, where n is the length of the input array.

