public class MinInsertions {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(minInsertions("(()))"));
    }

    public static int minInsertions(String s) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            if (s.charAt(i) == '(') {
                left++;
            } else if (s.charAt(i) == ')') {
                if (left > 0) {
                    left--;
                } else {
                    right++;
                }
            }
        }
        return left + right;
    }
}
```

In this implementation, we maintain two variables `left` and `right` to keep track of the number of unmatched '(' and ')' characters, respectively. We iterate through the string `s` and for each character, we check if it is a '(' or a ')'. If it is a '(', we increment the `left` variable. If it is a ')', we check if there is an unmatched '(' before it. If there is, we decrement the `left` variable. Otherwise, we increment the `right` variable.

Finally, we return the sum of `left` and `right` as the minimum number of insertions required to make the brackets balanced.

public int minInsertions(String s) {
    // Initialize a variable to store the minimum number of insertions
    int minInsertions = 0;
    // Initialize a stack to keep track of the opening brackets
    Stack<Character> openingBrackets = new Stack<>();

    // Iterate through the characters of the string
    for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
        // If the current character is a closing bracket
        if (c == ')') {
            // If the stack is empty, we need to insert a corresponding opening bracket
            if (openingBrackets.isEmpty()) {
                minInsertions++;
            } else {
                // Pop the top element from the stack
                openingBrackets.pop();
            }
        } else {
            // If the current character is an opening bracket
            if (c == '(') {
                // Push the opening bracket to the stack
                openingBrackets.push(c);
            }
        }
    }

    // If the stack is not empty, we need to insert closing brackets for the remaining opening brackets
    minInsertions += openingBrackets.size();

    return minInsertions;
}

